1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:06,400
You can run them. You can drive them. Build them. Fly them. Even eat them.

2
00:00:07,200 --> 00:00:12,380
There's one thing they all share. They're all made in the USA.

3
00:00:14,160 --> 00:00:19,480
Known for its large-scaled innovation, America has paved the way in the manufacturing world.

4
00:00:20,200 --> 00:00:22,184
When you can make things, you can do anything.

5
00:00:22,185 --> 00:00:25,160
It's that kind of destiny that America should always be striving for.

6
00:00:25,161 --> 00:00:32,160
Join us as we look at just a few of the things that are made in this country every day.

7
00:00:32,161 --> 00:00:36,160
It can be done, and we're doing it.

8
00:00:36,161 --> 00:00:40,160
It's Made in the USA on Modern Marvel.

9
00:00:40,161 --> 00:00:54,161
Over the last two centuries, the United States has carved its way into becoming one of the superpowers of production.

10
00:01:00,580 --> 00:01:05,240
However, with a globalized economy changing the face of the manufacturing world,

11
00:01:06,020 --> 00:01:08,340
made in the USA has taken on a new meaning.

12
00:01:10,160 --> 00:01:14,040
But the U.S. is still the number one manufacturer in the world,

13
00:01:15,240 --> 00:01:18,720
churning out over $1.5 trillion worth of goods a year.

14
00:01:19,720 --> 00:01:22,600
So what is the United States still making these days?

15
00:01:23,460 --> 00:01:25,780
The answer might surprise you.

16
00:01:26,600 --> 00:01:30,620
For 75 years, New Balance has been crafting athletic footwear

17
00:01:30,621 --> 00:01:33,980
for both the professional and everyday athlete.

18
00:01:36,480 --> 00:01:39,700
Originally founded in Boston in the early 1900s,

19
00:01:40,160 --> 00:01:49,852
New Balance has made a commitment to ensure at least 25% of its athletic shoes sold in North America are manufactured on U.S.

20
00:01:49,853 --> 00:01:50,553
soil.

21
00:01:51,760 --> 00:01:55,100
There are five domestic factories between Massachusetts and Maine.

22
00:01:55,640 --> 00:01:59,680
These five factories staff over 2,000 employees.

23
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:06,760
And one of those factories lies in the heart of Skohegan, Maine.

24
00:02:06,761 --> 00:02:12,580
In 1965, there were over 29,000 shoe jobs in Maine.

25
00:02:13,140 --> 00:02:16,340
Today, less than 10% of those jobs remain.

26
00:02:17,600 --> 00:02:20,040
It's important. It's not something you see every day.

27
00:02:20,180 --> 00:02:23,560
You know, a lot of stuff you see made in China, Japan, overseas.

28
00:02:24,080 --> 00:02:27,000
I think it's important that we still have something here in the United States

29
00:02:27,001 --> 00:02:28,600
keeping us busy, you know what I mean?

30
00:02:30,900 --> 00:02:32,320
It just gives me pride.

31
00:02:32,321 --> 00:02:35,360
You know, it's putting people to work.

32
00:02:35,361 --> 00:02:38,600
It's just a great thing.

33
00:02:38,601 --> 00:02:41,260
The factory is making New Balance's most popular

34
00:02:41,261 --> 00:02:44,040
technical shoe sold in North America,

35
00:02:44,041 --> 00:02:48,640
the men's size 10 1⁄2, model 993.

36
00:02:48,641 --> 00:02:51,940
By the end of the day, 3,000 pairs of shoes

37
00:02:51,941 --> 00:02:54,220
will be boxed and ready for the customers.

38
00:02:57,480 --> 00:03:02,320
The factory floor consists of seven separate stations,

39
00:03:02,321 --> 00:03:12,000
rolled goods, embroidery, pre-fit, computer stitching,

40
00:03:12,001 --> 00:03:17,760
manual stitching, sole preparation, and assembly area.

41
00:03:17,761 --> 00:03:19,480
The process of shoemaking is very interesting.

42
00:03:19,481 --> 00:03:23,500
We start right off with rolled goods coming in on the dock.

43
00:03:23,501 --> 00:03:25,320
Those goods go to the cutting tables

44
00:03:25,321 --> 00:03:26,920
where the cutters cut those parts.

45
00:03:26,921 --> 00:03:38,080
Over 30 components will build the top of the shoe, or what is called the upper.

46
00:03:38,081 --> 00:03:42,760
But before the upper is pieced together, the tongue makes a quick pit stop to the embroidering

47
00:03:42,761 --> 00:03:50,080
room, where the made in the USA label comes to life.

48
00:03:50,081 --> 00:03:56,700
These machines run at 800 RPMs, or 800 stitches per minute.

49
00:03:56,701 --> 00:04:05,200
At this rate, they spin over nine miles of thread each and every day.

50
00:04:05,201 --> 00:04:11,520
The upper begins to take form at the computerized stitching area.

51
00:04:11,521 --> 00:04:22,920
This is where the collar straps and signature ends are added.

52
00:04:22,921 --> 00:04:30,100
To ensure consistent stitch lines, these machines utilize what is called C technology.

53
00:04:30,101 --> 00:04:35,680
Using lighting and cameras, a machine will take a thousand pictures a second to follow

54
00:04:35,681 --> 00:04:37,620
the line of the shoe.

55
00:04:37,621 --> 00:04:41,300
So the machine will correct itself if there's any imperfections that we're making.

56
00:04:41,301 --> 00:04:44,040
That's very innovative machinery.

57
00:04:44,041 --> 00:04:49,440
After the core components are added, the shoe moves to manual stitching, or the closing

58
00:04:49,441 --> 00:04:50,440
area.

59
00:04:50,441 --> 00:04:56,940
This process relies on a more traditional sewing technique,

60
00:04:58,940 --> 00:05:01,440
the steady hands of the operator.

61
00:05:02,660 --> 00:05:05,540
This station speaks a language of its own.

62
00:05:06,840 --> 00:05:08,760
Here, they stitch the sprung tip,

63
00:05:09,740 --> 00:05:10,760
crimp the collar lining,

64
00:05:11,400 --> 00:05:12,880
and bonus clothes the heel.

65
00:05:14,680 --> 00:05:17,440
Only one size is made on any given day,

66
00:05:17,441 --> 00:05:24,400
ranging from a woman's four all the way up to a men's 18 quadruple E.

67
00:05:28,020 --> 00:05:32,900
Once the upper is completed, the shoe is steamed to form the heel and toe.

68
00:05:34,740 --> 00:05:38,480
At the same time, the sole is sprayed with hot melt cement,

69
00:05:38,840 --> 00:05:42,660
which is the gluing agent that keeps the sole and upper together.

70
00:05:44,340 --> 00:05:46,960
It is then placed in a flash activator.

71
00:05:47,440 --> 00:05:50,920
that applies quick, intense heat to ignite the adhesive.

72
00:05:54,780 --> 00:05:58,420
The sole and upper are then sealed and pressed.

73
00:05:58,421 --> 00:06:00,320
The insert is added.

74
00:06:00,321 --> 00:06:06,320
The shoe is laced, inspected, and boxed for shipment.

75
00:06:07,720 --> 00:06:09,560
The average New Balance athletic shoe

76
00:06:09,561 --> 00:06:12,900
takes 32 to 36 people to assemble.

77
00:06:14,500 --> 00:06:16,640
I've heard the analogy that we're not building watches

78
00:06:16,641 --> 00:06:19,900
many times and sometimes I wonder if that's true.

79
00:06:19,901 --> 00:06:22,720
The amount of technology that goes into this shoe,

80
00:06:22,721 --> 00:06:24,860
the quality that we're looking for,

81
00:06:24,861 --> 00:06:29,740
the tolerances of the stitching and the stitch lines.

82
00:06:29,741 --> 00:06:32,920
So as I said, I'm not sure we aren't building watches.

83
00:06:32,921 --> 00:06:35,880
It is pretty tight what we do every single day.

84
00:06:42,860 --> 00:06:45,800
Any one of the five U.S. New Balance plants

85
00:06:45,801 --> 00:06:49,660
produces between 3,000 to 7,000 pairs of shoes a day.

86
00:06:51,260 --> 00:06:54,620
For this company, much of the enjoyed success

87
00:06:54,621 --> 00:06:57,720
can be credited to the most unlikely of muses,

88
00:06:58,480 --> 00:06:59,180
the chicken.

89
00:07:02,160 --> 00:07:05,180
Boston, Massachusetts, 1906.

90
00:07:05,880 --> 00:07:07,380
English immigrant William Riley

91
00:07:07,381 --> 00:07:09,560
set out to create prescription footwear

92
00:07:09,561 --> 00:07:11,520
that would alleviate arch pain

93
00:07:11,521 --> 00:07:14,520
for those who spend their work days standing up.

94
00:07:14,521 --> 00:07:18,400
based on the structure of the three-pronged chicken foot,

95
00:07:18,880 --> 00:07:22,400
which Riley believed exemplified perfect balance.

96
00:07:23,560 --> 00:07:26,420
Fusing this concept with his knowledge of the human foot,

97
00:07:26,421 --> 00:07:29,180
the New Balance legacy was born.

98
00:07:31,480 --> 00:07:33,680
Over the next several decades,

99
00:07:34,240 --> 00:07:35,980
the New Balance shoe has developed

100
00:07:35,981 --> 00:07:38,500
into a complex piece of athletic footwear.

101
00:07:39,520 --> 00:07:42,500
But the race to perfection continues.

102
00:07:44,520 --> 00:07:47,560
At the New Balance Sports Lab in Lawrence, Massachusetts,

103
00:07:48,460 --> 00:07:52,680
engineers are working on the next generation of running shoes to be made in America.

104
00:07:54,180 --> 00:07:57,000
Utilizing the latest in motion sensor technology,

105
00:07:57,780 --> 00:08:00,600
a runner is monitored by eight high-speed cameras,

106
00:08:01,420 --> 00:08:03,380
taking 200 pictures a second.

107
00:08:04,560 --> 00:08:09,860
This information is then rendered into 3D animations for the engineers to analyze.

108
00:08:09,861 --> 00:08:16,200
We can utilize this information to calculate exactly the joint angles and segment motion

109
00:08:16,201 --> 00:08:18,760
we're looking to investigate.

110
00:08:18,761 --> 00:08:23,300
This approach allows the team to design a product that will better respond to a runner's

111
00:08:23,301 --> 00:08:28,480
needs.

112
00:08:28,481 --> 00:08:33,500
Our goal is to allow athletes to perform as best they can any given day of the week.

113
00:08:33,501 --> 00:08:38,040
We're going to make very small, minute changes that maybe the athlete doesn't even realize

114
00:08:38,041 --> 00:08:40,940
that's going to give them a second on an 800.

115
00:08:40,941 --> 00:08:44,580
A second is the difference between first and eighth.

116
00:08:44,581 --> 00:08:46,820
After a new shoe has been developed,

117
00:08:46,821 --> 00:08:49,380
it's sent to the Smash Lab.

118
00:08:49,381 --> 00:08:51,600
This machine's a servo-hydraulic unit

119
00:08:51,601 --> 00:08:54,780
that can produce very large quantities of force

120
00:08:54,781 --> 00:08:58,320
and can do so in very short bursts of time.

121
00:08:58,321 --> 00:09:01,220
In just a few minutes, the Mini Bionics unit

122
00:09:01,221 --> 00:09:06,600
can inflict the wear and tear of a three-hour marathon run.

123
00:09:06,601 --> 00:09:11,800
So the Mini Bionics unit basically allows us to produce anywhere from zero to 5,000 pounds of force.

124
00:09:12,520 --> 00:09:16,700
In this case, you're seeing the shoe being impacted 10 times per second.

125
00:09:18,840 --> 00:09:24,400
But when we're evaluating this type of product, we want to make sure that it's going through its utmost durability,

126
00:09:24,720 --> 00:09:28,280
making sure that nothing's going to break down over time or crack over time.

127
00:09:28,281 --> 00:09:33,160
So essentially, this is a pretty extreme test, but a pretty important test for our customers.

128
00:09:33,161 --> 00:09:36,160
The Smash Lab isn't necessarily the toughest exercise

129
00:09:36,161 --> 00:09:38,160
the New Balance brand will endure.

130
00:09:40,160 --> 00:09:44,160
For over two decades, New Balance has provided athletic footwear

131
00:09:44,161 --> 00:09:47,160
for every branch of the armed forces.

132
00:09:47,161 --> 00:09:53,160
Each pair is specialized with the Air Force, Army, Coast Guard,

133
00:09:53,161 --> 00:09:57,160
Navy, and Marine Corps emblems for the servicemen and women

134
00:09:57,161 --> 00:09:59,160
of the United States military.

135
00:10:03,160 --> 00:10:09,340
People should care about the fact that New Balance is making shoes domestically because

136
00:10:09,341 --> 00:10:16,940
it's unique, because so much we believe is not being done domestically anymore.

137
00:10:16,941 --> 00:10:20,600
We believe in the domestic workforce, we believe in their creativity, their innovation, their

138
00:10:20,601 --> 00:10:22,200
ability to meet demand.

139
00:10:22,201 --> 00:10:25,840
It can be done, and we're doing it.

140
00:10:25,841 --> 00:10:31,720
While New Balance was born to run, the Segway PT was built to ride.

141
00:10:31,721 --> 00:10:34,320
We're dissecting this gravity-defying unit

142
00:10:34,321 --> 00:10:37,520
to find out just how it stands on two wheels.

143
00:10:44,240 --> 00:10:47,540
For a product to be labeled Made in the USA,

144
00:10:47,541 --> 00:10:49,700
the Federal Trade Commission requires

145
00:10:49,701 --> 00:10:52,800
that at least 70% of the parts and labor

146
00:10:52,801 --> 00:10:56,520
come from the United States.

147
00:10:56,521 --> 00:10:59,680
Made in the USA will return on Modern Marvels.

148
00:11:01,720 --> 00:11:05,520
We now return to Made in the USA on Modern Marvels.

149
00:11:06,780 --> 00:11:11,220
The United States has long been known as the car manufacturing superpower.

150
00:11:12,220 --> 00:11:15,600
While this country is still building cars for the world over,

151
00:11:16,080 --> 00:11:18,940
some U.S. companies are rethinking the wheel.

152
00:11:20,220 --> 00:11:21,260
Washington, D.C.

153
00:11:21,940 --> 00:11:25,900
A tour group is gearing up for their first ride through the nation's capital

154
00:11:25,901 --> 00:11:28,780
via Segway personal transporters.

155
00:11:28,781 --> 00:11:31,780
Within minutes, they're off without a hitch.

156
00:11:31,781 --> 00:11:35,780
The Segway PT's uncanny ability to balance the most novice of riders

157
00:11:35,781 --> 00:11:37,780
is a technological breakthrough.

158
00:11:41,780 --> 00:11:44,780
I think the most interesting part technologically about the Segway

159
00:11:44,781 --> 00:11:50,780
is how sensitive the gyroscopes are to what pressure you put forward and back.

160
00:11:50,781 --> 00:11:53,780
Because I'm looking at you, so I feel like I should be closer to you,

161
00:11:53,781 --> 00:11:57,780
and that, you know, brain power just converts right to the feet power,

162
00:11:57,781 --> 00:12:00,380
and the Segway picks up on it absolutely intuitively.

163
00:12:00,480 --> 00:12:02,680
It's really, really cool.

164
00:12:02,780 --> 00:12:05,780
To understand the mind of the Segway PT,

165
00:12:05,880 --> 00:12:09,220
you would have to go to where it all begins,

166
00:12:09,320 --> 00:12:11,720
in Bedford, New Hampshire.

167
00:12:11,820 --> 00:12:14,200
Each and every unit sold around the world

168
00:12:14,300 --> 00:12:16,840
is built right here at the Segway headquarters.

169
00:12:20,440 --> 00:12:24,840
An American-made brand with out-of-this-world technology.

170
00:12:27,780 --> 00:12:39,020
Start to finish, the unit is completely assembled by a team of five in less than 30 minutes,

171
00:12:39,021 --> 00:12:44,540
with an average of 50 made a day.

172
00:12:44,541 --> 00:12:51,500
All parts come prefabricated from the U.S. and other countries around the world.

173
00:12:51,501 --> 00:12:56,600
A chassis begins its trek down the line and the Segway PT is ready to take form.

174
00:12:57,820 --> 00:13:00,300
The motors are the first to be installed.

175
00:13:04,560 --> 00:13:12,240
Followed by the control unit boards and sensors, these two components are considered the brains of the Segway PT.

176
00:13:15,120 --> 00:13:19,700
The gearbox is added, then the chassis enters the test station.

177
00:13:21,500 --> 00:13:27,340
Here the serial number is registered and the operating software is loaded and calibrated.

178
00:13:31,500 --> 00:13:34,780
Next the platform cover is placed over the chassis.

179
00:13:37,420 --> 00:13:41,500
This allows for the rider to communicate with the electrical boards and sensors.

180
00:13:44,140 --> 00:13:50,140
The hub of the handlebar or the pivot assembly is attached which feeds the steering commands

181
00:13:50,141 --> 00:13:51,940
to the processors in the control board.

182
00:13:54,600 --> 00:13:58,300
The assembly line ends with the wheel and battery installation.

183
00:14:03,580 --> 00:14:06,380
The handlebar is attached for the test drive,

184
00:14:06,381 --> 00:14:09,520
and the Segway PT is ready for shipment.

185
00:14:13,040 --> 00:14:16,020
This technology is credited to Dean Kamen,

186
00:14:16,320 --> 00:14:19,560
who originally set out to build a self-balancing wheelchair.

187
00:14:20,140 --> 00:14:26,880
capable of climbing stairs and allowing for the disabled to greet people at a standing eye level.

188
00:14:28,240 --> 00:14:32,700
After Kamen discovered his balancing system worked with a person standing up,

189
00:14:33,440 --> 00:14:35,400
the Segway transporter came to life.

190
00:14:37,000 --> 00:14:42,080
So how does this mobile unit pull off such a picture-perfect balancing act?

191
00:14:44,280 --> 00:14:48,200
The Segway works on a principle called dynamic stabilization.

192
00:14:48,201 --> 00:14:52,200
Sometimes it's more commonly referred to as the inverted pendulum,

193
00:14:52,201 --> 00:14:56,200
sort of like balancing a broomstick in your hand.

194
00:14:56,201 --> 00:15:00,200
So I stand upright, and when I start to lean forward,

195
00:15:00,201 --> 00:15:04,200
the Segway underneath me starts moving forward to match my movement,

196
00:15:04,201 --> 00:15:06,200
so it accelerates as I lean forward.

197
00:15:06,201 --> 00:15:10,200
The same is true of braking. When I lean back, the machine slows down.

198
00:15:13,200 --> 00:15:17,200
When the sensors that are attached to the leaning handlebars sense that I'm leaning one way or another,

199
00:15:17,201 --> 00:15:21,800
it responds in proportion to how I lean in order to sort of slalom,

200
00:15:21,900 --> 00:15:25,700
just as if I'm skiing or riding a skateboard.

201
00:15:25,800 --> 00:15:27,440
So how does it do all that?

202
00:15:27,540 --> 00:15:30,340
Well, let's start to take a look inside.

203
00:15:30,440 --> 00:15:34,420
J.D. Hinesman is one of Segway's principal engineers.

204
00:15:34,520 --> 00:15:38,080
He's been involved in the technology for over a decade.

205
00:15:38,180 --> 00:15:41,720
Because this system is balancing a person, we have...

206
00:15:41,820 --> 00:15:44,120
You'll see here everywhere that there's two,

207
00:15:44,220 --> 00:15:46,020
pretty much of everything electronic in here.

208
00:15:46,021 --> 00:15:48,997
here. So these are our control boards.

209
00:15:48,998 --> 00:15:53,540
For safety, every function in the Segway PT is duplicated,

210
00:15:54,180 --> 00:15:57,540
meaning there are two fully operational systems working in unison.

211
00:15:58,740 --> 00:16:04,100
This is called redundancy. For example, there are two motors driving each wheel.

212
00:16:05,220 --> 00:16:09,300
If one fails, the other is programmed to take full command,

213
00:16:09,301 --> 00:16:13,380
while signaling the board to bring the unit to a safe and complete stop.

214
00:16:13,381 --> 00:16:18,380
This prevents the Segway PT from ever losing its balance.

215
00:16:18,381 --> 00:16:22,380
That gravity-defying balance comes from a group of sensors

216
00:16:22,381 --> 00:16:25,380
that could fit in the palm of your hand.

217
00:16:25,381 --> 00:16:29,380
This unit that you see right in the middle here is what we call our BSA,

218
00:16:29,381 --> 00:16:31,380
or our balance sensor assembly.

219
00:16:31,381 --> 00:16:36,380
These components work together to figure out which way is up

220
00:16:36,381 --> 00:16:39,380
and figure out when the platform is level.

221
00:16:39,381 --> 00:16:46,940
level. This balancing assembly is a vast network of wires and sensors inspired by

222
00:16:46,941 --> 00:16:51,980
the human body. A person's equilibrium comes from the function of the inner ear

223
00:16:51,981 --> 00:16:58,220
which contains tiny patches of hair cells. When the head moves the cells are

224
00:16:58,221 --> 00:17:03,660
stimulated causing them to send messages to the brain. The brain then tells the

225
00:17:03,661 --> 00:17:08,360
body where it is in relation to gravity and earth in order for it to remain

226
00:17:08,361 --> 00:17:13,940
balanced. In the Segway, sensors respond to the rider's shift in weight.

227
00:17:13,941 --> 00:17:15,800
Signals are then sent to

228
00:17:15,801 --> 00:17:18,603
the processor, or the brain of the Segway.

229
00:17:18,604 --> 00:17:21,940
From there, operational commands go to the motors,

230
00:17:21,941 --> 00:17:27,808
which adjust the wheels to keep the platform level. The result?

231
00:17:27,809 --> 00:17:31,440
A fluid and seamless riding experience.

232
00:17:31,441 --> 00:17:37,240
With the balance in place, one question still remains.

233
00:17:38,140 --> 00:17:39,300
How fast does it go?

234
00:17:41,180 --> 00:17:44,520
The Segway PTs can go up to 12 and a half miles an hour,

235
00:17:44,760 --> 00:17:47,860
which is about three to four times a walking pace.

236
00:17:48,320 --> 00:17:51,560
It's roughly the equivalent of an average person sprinting.

237
00:17:52,140 --> 00:17:54,180
And the reason we chose 12 and a half miles an hour

238
00:17:54,181 --> 00:17:55,840
was because we designed the Segway PT

239
00:17:55,841 --> 00:17:57,400
to fit into a pedestrian environment.

240
00:17:57,920 --> 00:18:01,120
So operating on sidewalks with other people who might be walking,

241
00:18:01,121 --> 00:18:06,060
so we chose that speed limit so that the vehicle would integrate more easily into that environment.

242
00:18:08,760 --> 00:18:13,340
Because of its smooth glide, the Segway PT has been adopted

243
00:18:13,341 --> 00:18:16,420
to alleviate a different set of commuting obstacles.

244
00:18:19,460 --> 00:18:24,120
SEGS for Vets is an organization that is utilizing the unit's intuitive technology

245
00:18:24,121 --> 00:18:29,080
to help veterans who have sustained catastrophic injuries during combat operations.

246
00:18:29,081 --> 00:18:31,080
Yeah, very nice.

247
00:18:31,081 --> 00:18:33,580
And what's great is that through legislation

248
00:18:33,581 --> 00:18:36,580
that we've been able to pass, the Segway

249
00:18:36,581 --> 00:18:38,580
is now considered a fully-fledged wheelchair.

250
00:18:42,580 --> 00:18:46,080
A group of recipients are being trained on how to best

251
00:18:46,081 --> 00:18:49,580
navigate the Segway PT through their new set of challenges.

252
00:18:49,581 --> 00:18:56,580
challenges.

253
00:18:56,680 --> 00:19:03,680
SEGS4Vets has donated over 500 Segway units to eligible veterans.

254
00:19:03,860 --> 00:19:09,820
An unintended benefit, born from the very technology Dean Kamen created to help those

255
00:19:09,821 --> 00:19:11,820
with disabilities.

256
00:19:11,821 --> 00:19:15,070
It changes everything about it, and you don't mind being on a Segway.

257
00:19:15,071 --> 00:19:16,200
You don't feel disabled,

258
00:19:16,201 --> 00:19:17,940
And people don't look at you as disabled.

259
00:19:17,941 --> 00:19:21,800
They're always just looking up at you now.

260
00:19:21,801 --> 00:19:24,200
The Segway just became an extension of my legs.

261
00:19:26,200 --> 00:19:29,240
And it's given me back a lot of things

262
00:19:29,241 --> 00:19:30,280
that I thought I'd lost.

263
00:19:39,060 --> 00:19:44,200
This balanced technology is also being exported.

264
00:19:44,201 --> 00:19:47,360
Marathon Robotics has applied the technology

265
00:19:47,361 --> 00:19:51,160
in creating live-fire exercises for the Australian military.

266
00:19:55,180 --> 00:19:59,360
A teleoperator can control a fleet of free-ranging targets

267
00:19:59,361 --> 00:20:02,960
by designing a game scenario on a computer.

268
00:20:02,961 --> 00:20:05,240
This allows the conductor to simulate

269
00:20:05,241 --> 00:20:10,080
unpredictable movements for the soldier's point of view.

270
00:20:10,081 --> 00:20:18,880
But in the broader scope, Segway's goal is still to help the world rethink the way it commutes.

271
00:20:19,600 --> 00:20:26,520
The next generation of U.S.-made Segway transporters has been unveiled, such as the Puma.

272
00:20:28,060 --> 00:20:34,460
Utilizing the Segway sensory technology, this transporter steers with a drive-by wire interface,

273
00:20:35,600 --> 00:20:37,940
eliminating all mechanical controls.

274
00:20:37,941 --> 00:20:46,700
another is the envy which is designed to perform autonomous mobility leaving the driving to the

275
00:20:46,701 --> 00:20:55,760
machine in collaboration with General Motors these visionary prototypes are slated for a 2030 release

276
00:20:55,761 --> 00:21:04,880
the Segway PT is an American-made machine that defies conventional design and a company that

277
00:21:04,881 --> 00:21:13,040
pushes forward in creating the transportation of the future today.

